--TEST--
A `Core\Env` name refuses a `tainted` argument and every value it answers with is one — `rule:security/sink-predicate`, `rule:security/tainted-qualifier`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:security/sink-predicate`: a parameter whose content becomes an instruction refuses a
// qualified argument. `Core\Env::get`'s name is one — it names what the
// runtime must hand back — so a program cannot let untrusted input choose
// which variable it reads. `Core\IO`'s path parameters are the same rule over
// a less sensitive store.
tainted string $chosen = "NVS_CASE_ONE";
var $leak = Core\Env::get($chosen);

// `rule:security/tainted-qualifier`: the environment is outside the program's own text, so every
// value is `tainted` whatever the operator put there. It does not narrow to a
// plain `string` on the way out, which is what makes the sinks downstream
// still have to launder it.
foreach (Core\Env::all() as string $name => string $value) {
    echo $name, $value, "\n";
}
--EXPECT-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
  --> case.nvs:8:27
  |
8 | var $leak = Core\Env::get($chosen);
  |                           ^^^^^^^ this is `tainted string`

error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `tainted string`
   --> case.nvs:14:45
   |
14 | foreach (Core\Env::all() as string $name => string $value) {
   |                                             ^^^^^^^^^^^^^ this is `tainted string`

error: aborting due to 2 errors
